{
  "id": "isomorphic-strings",
  "questionId": "isomorphic-strings",
  "slug": "isomorphic-strings",
  "title": "Isomorphic Strings",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Hash map",
  "patterns": [
    "hash map"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "checking every pair of positions is wasteful - can you decide validity by looking at each position only once?",
    "what if you kept a map from each character in s to the character it's already been mapped to in t, and checked consistency as you go?",
    "you need the mapping to be consistent in BOTH directions - s-to-t and t-to-s - otherwise two different source letters could collapse onto the same target letter",
    "at each position, check (or set) both maps: s[i] -> t[i] and t[i] -> s[i]; if either map already has a different value recorded, it's not isomorphic"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "only checking the mapping in one direction (s to t), missing cases where two source characters map to the same target",
    "using a single map and overwriting previous mappings instead of detecting the conflict",
    "forgetting to handle a character mapping to itself as a valid (trivial) case"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given two strings, `s` and `t`, of the same length.\n\n**Determine whether the characters in `s` can be consistently replaced\nto get `t`.**\n\nEvery occurrence of a given character in `s` must always map to the\nsame character in `t`, and no two different characters in `s` may map\nto the same character in `t`.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^4",
      "t.length == s.length",
      "s and t consist of any valid ASCII character"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a character maps to itself",
      "two different source characters trying to map to the same target character",
      "single character strings"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"egg\", t = \"add\"",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "e maps to a, g maps to d, consistently throughout"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"foo\", t = \"bar\"",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "the two o's in foo would need to map to both a and r, which isn't consistent"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"paper\", t = \"title\"",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": ""
      }
    ],
